/* ==========================================================================
   TYPOGRAPHY

   Four voices, and they differ by more than size. Titles are lettered - set
   apart, meant to be looked at. Questions are spoken at normal weight, because
   somebody has to read them and answer. Hints are small and quiet and exist to
   be glanced at rather than read. Sections are the quietest of all: they are
   signposts, not content.
   ========================================================================== */

/* Nothing down the sides of a label, and this is the rule the whole left edge
   of the interface hangs on.

   Unity's theme puts a horizontal margin on a label the same way it puts one on
   a control. Every control in this theme has had that turned off, and until now
   the labels had not - which was the wrong half to leave, because a label in a
   form row is not just text sitting there: it is the thing the control after it
   is pushed off. .field__label is a fixed 200 wide, so a margin on it put the
   dropdown at 200 plus the margin twice, while the hint underneath - which
   states its own margin-left of exactly 200 and so overwrote the theme's -
   stayed where it was told.

   That is the gap. Everything in the control column at one x, the hints a few
   pixels to the left of it, and the same few pixels everywhere because the
   thing doing the pushing was the same in every row.

   Said of every label rather than of that one, because the same margin was also
   holding the panel's own left edge open: a title, a hint and a section were all
   sitting a couple of pixels inside the padding while a button beside them met
   it. Every deliberate horizontal margin in the interface - the hint's indent to
   the control column, the gap before a slider's value, the roster's spacing - is
   written in a file imported after this one and still wins. */

.unity-label {
    margin-left: 0;
    margin-right: 0;
}


.title {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font-size-l);

    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);

    margin-bottom: var(--space-l);

    white-space: normal;

    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}


/* A title with a line under it that belongs to it. The title's own bottom
   margin is the gap to whatever the screen holds next, and a subtitle is not
   next - it is the second half of the same sentence. Nothing between them at
   all, so the two lines are read as one thing; the gap that used to be here
   moves below the pair, where it separates them from the screen. */

.title--subtitled {
    margin-bottom: 0;
}


/* The name of the game, which is the one string in the interface allowed to
   behave like a picture. Wide enough that the letters stop being a word and
   start being a shape. */

.title--display {
    /* Brighter than any text in the game, because it is not text. Set at this
       size with the letters this far apart, the name reads as the thing on the
       cover rather than as the first line of a menu, and the resting text
       colour was leaving it looking like a heading somebody forgot to finish. */
    color: var(--text-display);

    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);

    /* One line, whatever happens. The name is set large and spaced out, so a
       wrap does not read as a second line of a title - it reads as the title
       not fitting. It is placed on the screen rather than inside the column
       now, so there is room to the right for it to run. */
    white-space: nowrap;

    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wider);

    /* No rule under it, and nothing holding space for one.

       The line was the cheapest way to say this is a masthead and not the first
       row of something, back when the name sat at the top of a column with the
       menu directly beneath it. There is nothing beneath it now: the name is
       placed on the screen, the column runs the full height past it, and what
       follows it down the frame is empty until the actions at the floor. A rule
       divides two things, and there was only ever one.

       The padding and the margin went with it. Both existed to hold the name
       off that line and off whatever came after; in an absolutely placed
       masthead holding one label they were measuring a gap nobody can see. */
}


.question {
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: var(--font-size-m);

    white-space: normal;

    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}


.hint {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);

    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);

    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);

    white-space: normal;

    -unity-text-align: middle-center;
}


/* A hint that is the second line of a title rather than a note under a
   control. It carries the whole gap the pair needs underneath it - the title's
   own, and then some, because two lines read as one block need more air below
   them than one line did.

   Written after .hint and not before it. The two weigh the same, so the later
   one wins, and on the wrong side of that line this rule was handing its
   margin straight back to the one it exists to override. */

.subtitle {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-l);
}


.section {
    color: var(--text-dim);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);

    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);

    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
}
